Native 16-bit SYCL GEMM source proposal v1
=======================================

STATUS: UNBUILT, UNTESTED, UNRUN. No production files were edited. Main owns
full patch review, integration, builds, tests, profiling and GPU measurement.
Exact Strata base: 375a6d6ee905859ef176997262e3baef6e739ad2.
This is a finite producer component; the long-prefill path is not converted.

Contents and integration
------------------------

`touchedbase/` contains exact Git bytes for the four modified existing files.
`proposedfiles/` contains those four candidate files plus the two new files.
`source.diff` is a standard unified patch generated only from that base, never
from the mutable live worktree. Hashes and absent-at-base entries are in
`manifest.json`. Main must review the complete patch and reconcile newer live
changes before integration. Do not overwrite live files from these snapshots.

The root CMakeLists.txt includes cmake/sycl_backend.cmake when
STRATA_ENABLE_SYCL=ON. That backend selects strata_prefill; the proposal adds
src/prefill/sycl/gemm16.cpp to this library. The executable strata links this
library. The new manual sycl_gemm16_test target is EXCLUDE_FROM_ALL, outside the
STRATA_SYCL_TESTS block, with no add_test/automatic execution. Compiler options
retain -fno-fast-math and -ffp-contract=off. No compile/configure was attempted;
actual compiler/device lowering and the active build directory remain Main's
verification obligations. Old gemm.cpp definitions and oneMKL calls are unchanged.

API and mathematical contract
-----------------------------

Under STRATA_ENABLE_SYCL, Gemm adds f16_out16 / bf16_out16 with
(X, W, Y, T, N, K, output_bf16=false, ldy=0, beta=0). X[T,K] and W[N,K]
are row-major uint16 bit representations in the named input format. Y[T,N]
is FP16 or BF16 according to output_bf16. The same-format choice for both
inputs is deliberate. The old float-output methods remain the FP32 reference.

Each call computes X * transpose(W) with native typed half or native typed
sycl::ext::oneapi::bfloat16 joint matrices, an explicitly float accumulator,
and an explicitly float FMA for beta*old_Y + accumulator. Only the resulting
scalar is rounded to the selected 16-bit output representation. beta=0 never
reads old Y, including NaN bits. No full global FP32 output, global scratch,
CPU GEMM, oneMKL fallback, dequantization change or range clamp is introduced.
NaNs/infinities follow the SYCL native operations and conversion; finite-input
range/underflow behavior is a qualification question tested by the fixture.

Initial geometry accepts arbitrary positive T (including 1, 3 and 17), N/K
positive multiples of 16, ldy=0 or ldy>=N, finite beta including 0/1 and other
values. Negative ldy is rejected. Odd N/K explicitly throw before submission;
Main may initially restrict production dimensions to these documented bounds.
Output padding is untouched. Rectangle counts, byte sizes, address ranges and
global launch size are checked before submission. No per-call allocations,
queue waits or environment reads/writes are in the new GEMM; null stream uses
the existing synchronous finish convention, while explicit stream is async.
CPU profiling uses the existing gemm_submit region around submission only.

The workgroup is 64 items / four size-16 subgroups and computes 16x32 outputs.
Each subgroup holds one 8x16 float matrix accumulator; matrix K is 16. A
single K=32 stage uses 16*32 input elements plus 32*32 weight elements, both
16 bit: exactly 3072 bytes of aligned SLM per group, no SLM float outputs.
W's row-major tile is read as column-major B[K,N], preserving contiguous K
reads. Row/column/K loads are individually guarded into zero-filled SLM.
Both barriers and all subgroup matrix operations are converged. Tail stores
are predicated only within the coordinate callback. This conservative design
makes no occupancy or performance claim; repeated whole-dependency-path
measurements and numerical checks are still required.

The first valid call caches an Intel-GPU matrix-combination capability query
on its Gemm instance; supported C and D must both be FP32 at 8x16x16. It also
checks subgroup/workgroup/SLM bounds. Half output needs fp16 support. There is
no software fallback if unsupported. Host calls on the same Gemm must be
serialized, consistent with init/rebind ownership. Context/stream ownership
cannot be replaced while an instance is active. Tuple support does not prove
successful compiler lowering for all input/layout variants: Main must build
and execute all four typed I/O variants.

Registered device spans (necessary narrow runtime addition)
----------------------------------------------------------

Existing validate_spans checks alignment/overlap/address arithmetic, but does
not know allocation extents. The proposal therefore additionally registers
MemoryKind::Device Allocation extents and runtime identity in runtime.cpp,
including core arenas and cudaMalloc adapter allocations. validate_device_span
checks containment and the queue's context/device before GEMM submission;
ordinary host pointers, host USM and independently allocated foreign device
USM are rejected. No new driver/Level Zero dependency or compiler dependency
is added. Two-byte global pointer alignment is supported through scalar bit
loads/stores and separately aligned typed SLM. Inputs may alias each other;
Y cannot overlap either input, including the covered strided output interval.

Registry lookup does not wait, submit or access tensor contents. Allocation
moves preserve the registered address. Deallocation retains existing checked
drain behavior, frees and retires the address under the registry mutex. The
registry is initialized before runtime_for's static runtime owners to keep
teardown ordering valid. Direct Allocation construction also initializes the
registry before its owner completes construction. Caller ownership still
must keep buffers alive until explicit queues finish; validation does not
provide a lease against concurrent destruction or synchronize other queues.
This small addition changes allocation bookkeeping for all SYCL allocations;
Main must review lifecycle/concurrency and run the existing runtime/compat and
FP32 GEMM fixtures as well as the new test before adoption.

Source-only validation fixture
------------------------------

The independent host oracle decodes IEEE fields to double and implements RNE
with explicit integer/fraction arithmetic; it does not use SYCL or Strata's
16-bit conversion helpers. All four format pairs are checked separately.
--smoke covers 68 numerical cases: 1/3/17 rows, N/K 16/32/48, padded/odd
strides and ldy=0, beta 0/1/.5/-.25/.1, cancellation, signed small values,
FP16/BF16 subnormals, normal boundaries and maximum finite inputs. Cross-format
range conversions explicitly require signed infinity where the rounded result
overflows. Subnormal preservation is checked; a hardware flush is not silently
accepted. Numerical comparison permits the FP32 reduction/FMA bound based on
sum(abs(products)) plus half one output-format ULP, with different FP16/BF16
ULPs. This is a component criterion, not bitwise full-model acceptance.

The K=48 cancellation fixture has consecutive 16-wide sums +32768, +16,
-32768, whose FP32 result is 16. Rounding the matrix accumulator to half or
BF16 between those updates loses the middle 16. Input and output buffers have
17-word guards, deliberately placing the live pointer at 2 mod 4; complete
input/guard copies and all output stride guards are checked after execution.
Prelaunch rejects cover shape/stride overflow, unsupported N/K, finite-beta
validation, missing initialization, nulls, byte misalignment, overlap, ordinary
host/host-USM/foreign-device-USM pointers and insufficient registered extents.
Allocation move and stale-range rejection are checked. A separately wrong
queue context/device and concurrent ownership stress are not exercised by
this one-compute-queue fixture; Main must assess them as runtime changes.

--bench takes one of six named shapes, one input/output format pair and 1..3
repetitions. Shapes are T=16/160/8192 with (N,K)=(1280,2560) or (2560,640).
Device tensors are bounded below 96 MiB; uploads/readback use one host tensor
at a time. Each process owns one used compute queue, one warmup, and a bounded
sample count. Every completion wall-time sample is emitted separately; timings
include validation/submission/wait, and exclude uploads/readback. Constant,
exactly representable inputs allow every output and guard to be verified.
No performance or correctness result exists yet. No raw diagnostic artifacts
were created and no cleanup was performed.

Future commands for Main only — ALL UNRUN
-----------------------------------------

Use Main's reviewed build directory/toolchain recipe and serialized GPU lock.
No configure command is proposed because Main owns the actual current resolver.

  cmake --build <reviewed-sycl-build> --target sycl_gemm16_test -j <owned-jobs>

First debug invocation inherits Main's qualified frozen pre-exec environment;
set these exact additional values in the native child's environment before
exec, never by setenv/unsetenv after SYCL/provider initialization:

  UR_ENABLE_LAYERS=UR_LAYER_TRACING,UR_LAYER_PARAMETER_VALIDATION
  UR_LOG_TRACING='level:info;flush:info;output:stderr'
  <reviewed-sycl-build>/sycl_gemm16_test --smoke

Use a finite deadline (e.g. 120 s), private output directory and 64 MiB maximum
raw text-log budget under Main's supervisor. Preserve build/source identities,
exact effective environment and outcome. Trace settings affect timings; run
bench in a separate clean process whose environment was frozen before exec.

  <reviewed-sycl-build>/sycl_gemm16_test --bench 0 fp16 fp16 3
  <reviewed-sycl-build>/sycl_gemm16_test --bench 1 fp16 bf16 3
  <reviewed-sycl-build>/sycl_gemm16_test --bench 2 bf16 bf16 3
  <reviewed-sycl-build>/sycl_gemm16_test --bench 5 bf16 fp16 3

These heavy commands are examples, not a completed sweep or measured result.
Main should first qualify every format with smoke, inspect device lowering and
fault evidence, and only then select representative shapes with finite owned
deadlines (e.g. 180 s per bench). Retain individual samples and failure status.
After source adoption, Main separately integrates direct 16-bit consumers in
prefill.cpp; this proposal touches neither it nor postops.cpp, decode, KV,
GGUF formats, GDN state precision, CUDA/HIP or model dispatch thresholds.

Provenance
----------

See provenance.json for immutable hashes and distinctions between original
implementation, local own API patterns and external architecture/API references.
The vLLM dtype policy is a design reference only: half/BF16 storage and float
accumulator/epilogue. Root Apache-2.0 and the sibling BSD-3-Clause notice were
checked. No vLLM/CUTLASS templates or foreign tests/helpers were copied, adapted
or included. Installed Intel 2026.1 headers were consulted for API signatures;
their LLVM SPDX and Intel modification notices are recorded without copying
their implementation. All newly authored files use the repository MIT license.
XeStrata source/helpers/tests/archive proposals were not consulted or used.
